WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.760
Director Paul Thomas Anderson's 1997&nbsp;
classic Boogie Nights takes the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.320
on a vivid ride through the golden years of&nbsp;
the porn industry. The story behind the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.880
however, is almost as wild. This is&nbsp;
the untold truth of Boogie Nights.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:21.760
Paul Thomas Anderson's father, Ernie "Ghoulardi"&nbsp;
Anderson, began his career in the 1960s as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:27.040
Cleveland television horror movie host. However,&nbsp;
he eventually found success as a Hollywood voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.840
artist, counting comedians, actors and&nbsp;
other Hollywood types among his friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:38.400
Ernie had worked with Tim Conway at WJW-TV in&nbsp;
Cleveland in the '60s. Conway left to star in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.440
McHale's Navy, but eventually Ernie joined&nbsp;
him in Los Angeles, and the rest is history.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.240
According to Esquire, Ernie eventually bought&nbsp;
a big house in the San Fernando Valley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.520
where he would frequently play&nbsp;
host to his various funny friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:56.240
like Conway and actor Harvey Korman — both&nbsp;
of whom had a "flair for twisted humor."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:01.920
For Paul's 12th birthday, Ernie bought him a&nbsp;
Betamax camcorder. With the camcorder in hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.800
Paul went on to make vignettes that followed his&nbsp;
father and his zany Hollywood friends. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.240
to Esquire, Paul's father likely inspired&nbsp;
Burt Reynolds' Jack Horner in Boogie Nights.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.040
There were other inspirations as well. As&nbsp;
Paul explained to the Philadelphia City Paper,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.920
"[Growing up in the San Fernando Valley], porn was&nbsp;
always around. There were these industrial-looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.560
buildings, with no signage, but you'd see people&nbsp;
going in and out; you knew what they were doing."

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.680
Anderson has been described by many of the&nbsp;
people involved in Boogie Nights as looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.480
like a kid when they first met him, which makes&nbsp;
sense since he was 26 at the time that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.760
looking to helm his first big Hollywood feature.&nbsp;
However, Anderson had already made one movie about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.560
Dirk and the porn industry when he was still in&nbsp;
high school. That film, The Dirk Diggler Story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.880
was a visual first draft for what he&nbsp;
would finally realize in Boogie Nights.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.240
According to Grantland.com's&nbsp;
oral history of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:59.280
Paul Thomas Anderson claims to have had an&nbsp;
epiphany about the name "Dirk Diggler" at 17.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.760
He then wrote the name on a piece of paper, and&nbsp;
began putting together a student film using a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.480
camcorder and a Steadicam. Anderson pulled&nbsp;
together his friends to play Dirk, Reed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.920
and various others in the low-budget film. He did&nbsp;
have one star involved, though: his dad, Ernie.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.640
Back in the '90s, John Lesher was&nbsp;
a powerful talent agent. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:22.560
his clients was Harmony Korine, a&nbsp;
talented young writer whose career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.160
began when he wrote Kids at age 19. As&nbsp;
Deadline explained in a 2013 article,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.800
Lesher championed Korine for years while&nbsp;
an agent at UTA and Endeavor, and Lesher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.600
continued that support as a producer. Yet Lesher&nbsp;
had another wunderkind client at the time:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.880
Paul Thomas Anderson. Michael de Luca, an exec&nbsp;
at New Line Cinema at the time, told Grantland,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.720
"I was talking with John [Lesher] about the&nbsp;
movie 'Kids.' I passed when he brought it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:51.360
to New Line [...] Just as a joke, I said to him,&nbsp;
'Don't let me pass on something this cool again,'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.520
and later [...] John said, 'Remember when you&nbsp;
said don't let you pass on something cool?'"

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.880
Lesher then described in the article&nbsp;
how he met de Luca for drinks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.560
gave him the script for Boogie Nights,&nbsp;
and the next day they were in business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.200
According to entertainment journalist Sharon&nbsp;
Waxman, another reason for de Luca to pick up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.880
Boogie Nights may have been that he was a, quote,&nbsp;
"bad boy" at the time. Waxman told Grantland,

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.840
"[Michael de Luca] loved that&nbsp;
life and he lived that life."

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.560
Whether it was good agenting or simply a case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.960
party boy meets party script, it seems as&nbsp;
destined as Dirk Diggler for greatness.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.320
At 23, Anderson got into the Sundance&nbsp;
Film Festival with his short film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.040
Cigarettes and Coffee. The following year,&nbsp;
Anderson came back to the Sundance Lab and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.560
expanded on Cigarettes and Coffee&nbsp;
with his first feature, Hard Eight.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.120
Paul Thomas Anderson titled it Sydney when&nbsp;
he first conceived the idea in high school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.200
The idea for the film came after he&nbsp;
watched a short Philip Baker Hall scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:51.120
as a consigliere named Sydney in Midnight Run.&nbsp;
Sydney was Anderson's working title for the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.920
and he was infuriated when his backers took&nbsp;
final cut and the name away from him. The movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.520
had a budget of $3 million and starred Hall and&nbsp;
John C. Reilly as the leads, Gwyneth Paltrow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:06.640
Samuel Jackson as top supporting actors, plus&nbsp;
Philip Seymour Hoffman early in his career.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.840
For a debut film from a 25-year-old kid, Hard&nbsp;
Eight notched great reviews and paved the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.240
for Anderson's first studio feature. The&nbsp;
budget for Boogie Nights was $15 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.280
and there were more actors and sets, but&nbsp;
Hall, Reilly and Hoffman all returned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.120
work with Anderson on a movie that would&nbsp;
help propel the careers of all four.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.800
Despite the fact that the movie was an at&nbsp;
times depressing story about the porn industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.200
a lot of actors were circling the script.&nbsp;
According to Grantland's oral history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.840
Drew Barrymore was Roller Girl at one point.&nbsp;
The role of director/producer Jack Horner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.000
however, was offered to a lot of big&nbsp;
talent. Bill Murray, Harvey Keitel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.880
Sydney Pollack and Albert Brooks were supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.920
all offered the role. But Paul Thomas Anderson&nbsp;
discussed it extensively with Warren Beatty.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:55.840
"I got to know Warren many years&nbsp;
ago, about 20 years ago when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.240
going to do 'Boogie Nights.' I sent him&nbsp;
the script and asked him to be in it."

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.720
As Anderson said in an interview with Charlie&nbsp;
Rose, Warren had shown a lot of interest in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.960
playing the part of Jack Horner. However,&nbsp;
Anderson ultimately made a realization that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.880
Beatty didn't necessarily want to play&nbsp;
the director. Anderson told Grantland,

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.680
"I started to figure out that Warren really wanted&nbsp;
to play Dirk Diggler (Mark Wahlberg). 'You don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.840
really want to play Jack Horner. You want to&nbsp;
be the kid in this movie.' He said, 'Yeah.'"

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.400
Horner eventually went to Burt Reynolds, and&nbsp;
it's hard now imagining anybody else playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.240
it. Though it appears that Reynolds brought&nbsp;
quite a few challenges to the production.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:36.480
In an interview with GQ, Burt Reynolds was asked&nbsp;
if he'd work with Anderson again. His answer?

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.440
"I don't think so.&nbsp;
Personality-wise, we didn't fit."

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.280
According to the Grantland oral history,&nbsp;
there was even a moment when Anderson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:47.840
gave Reynolds perhaps less respect than he&nbsp;
deserved, and it supposedly came to blows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:52.400
Burt Reynolds clarified to Conan O'Brien that&nbsp;
he hadn't punched Anderson, but wanted to.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.520
"No, I didn't want to hit him in the&nbsp;
face — I just wanted to hit him."

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:02.320
According to The Hollywood Reporter's obituary&nbsp;
for the actor, Reynolds was so unhappy with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:07.440
film and his place in it that he fired his agent&nbsp;
after seeing it for the first time. It was ironic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.360
then, that he won his only film Golden&nbsp;
Globe for his turn as Jack Horner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.560
Plus he was nominated for the&nbsp;
best supporting actor Oscar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.240
although he eventually lost to Robin&nbsp;
Williams for his turn in Good Will Hunting.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.120
One of the most intense scenes in "Boogie&nbsp;
Nights" occurs toward the end when Dirk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:28.240
Reed, played by John C. Reilly, and Todd Parker,&nbsp;
played by Thomas Jane, are sitting in the living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.040
room of a crazy rich man's house, looking to&nbsp;
rip him off by selling him some fake dope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.520
In the scene, there's a random boy just walking&nbsp;
around throwing firecrackers at the floor.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.200
Many of the cast recalled how that was&nbsp;
the craziest scene in the whole film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:45.280
and in large part that was due to the&nbsp;
unexpected randomness of the constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.080
mini-explosions. Director Robert Downey&nbsp;
Sr. said in Grantland's oral history,

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:55.040
"That idea came from a scene in my movie 'Putney&nbsp;
Swope.' It's one of Paul's favorites. My thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:58.880
was the Asian guy who's throwing around&nbsp;
firecrackers and just doesn't say a word."

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:04.080
Another inspiration, though, was Anderson's&nbsp;
father. According to Esquire, Ernie Anderson liked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.880
"tossing firecrackers around." So there you&nbsp;
have it: Two showbiz fathers with a fondness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.080
for firecrackers inspired one of the most insane&nbsp;
scenes in a movie full of intense sequences.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.520
A few characters, or at least character names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.080
made it to Boogie Nights from The Dirk&nbsp;
Diggler Story. One of those is Reed Rothchild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.400
Dirk's best friend in the porn industry. In&nbsp;
the original, though, Reed was very different.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.520
Reed was played by Eddie Dalcour, a bodybuilder&nbsp;
who was also Dirk's lover in that original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.640
iteration. Dalcour features prominently in the&nbsp;
student film since he was Dirk's best friend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:40.000
There's even a scene where they're both&nbsp;
together in a hot tub. While the romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.440
element of the Dirk/Reed relationship&nbsp;
didn't make it into Boogie Nights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.640
one Dalcour item did — Reed's hot tub poem.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.200
According to the oral history, Dalcour was&nbsp;
constantly trying to get Anderson to crack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.360
up on the set and was even invited to be&nbsp;
in Boogie Nights. However, by that point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.520
Dalcour had begun his career as a Christian&nbsp;
minister, which would've been tough to square&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.560
with a film about drugs and porn. One of his&nbsp;
improvisations was the poem that John C. Reilly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.320
recites as Reed while in the hot tub with Dirk.&nbsp;
It's a poem about love and honey trees and bees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:15.520
one so bad yet so amazing because of how well&nbsp;
it sets up the duo's rock solid friendship.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.360
"I love you. You love me.&nbsp;
Going down the sugar tree."

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:22.240
"Did you write that?"

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.920
"Yeah. I write songs, too."

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:28.960
Anderson had been making movies since he&nbsp;
was 12, and some of those early ones were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.440
actually scripted films. In fact&nbsp;
Anderson's early films all seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:37.840
to march toward what he would eventually&nbsp;
realize in Hard Eight and Boogie Nights.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:41.200
Obviously The Dirk Diggler&nbsp;
Story is a major stepping stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:46.320
from the tale of Dirk's rise and fall to the fact&nbsp;
that recording "The Touch" was featured in both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:51.360
as an example of Dirk's failed second career.&nbsp;
But according to Esquire, it becomes striking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.400
how much of these films began when Anderson was&nbsp;
still too young to actually buy pornography.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.880
Esquire mentions how Anderson first told a&nbsp;
teacher he was going to make a movie called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.520
Sydney based solely on the snippet of&nbsp;
Philip Baker Hall he'd seen in Midnight Run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.000
As a young teen, however, he made a&nbsp;
Miami Vice parody called Brock Landers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.920
which went on to be the name of Dirk's&nbsp;
monumental X-rated action series.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.080
"Does this character have a name?"

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.640
"His name is Brock Landers."

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:26.240
Anderson also made a Young Guns parody&nbsp;
called Young Buns, with erotic undertones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:30.080
If nothing else, Anderson was a&nbsp;
student of film from a very early age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.440
and didn't just want to watch them&nbsp;
but wanted to be part of them as well.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.360
John Holmes was a very successful adult film&nbsp;
actor known for having a heavy drug addiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.160
and, eventually, his possible role in the&nbsp;
Wonderland Murders. According to Esquire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:48.400
Paul Thomas Anderson told a friend in high&nbsp;
school that the idea for his next movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:54.000
was inspired by the sad life of Holmes. In a&nbsp;
1997 interview with Philadelphia City Paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.680
Anderson explained how Dirk's career&nbsp;
was very similar to that of Holmes:

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.000
"With the '80s and the drugs, everything's&nbsp;
taken a toll on him. That happened in John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.080
Holmes' life and it was reflected in&nbsp;
the character he played [Johnny Wadd].&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.400
We did the same thing with Dirk, and in the&nbsp;
movie I blame the drugs and ego-building."

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:15.360
In that same interview, he also specifically&nbsp;
mentions the early Johnny Wadd films as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.760
examples of great storytelling in porn.&nbsp;
There was also a John Holmes documentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.520
called Exhausted that was one of the&nbsp;
inspirations for Anderson to shoot The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:27.520
Dirk Diggler Story as a mockumentary.&nbsp;
It was such a big inspiration that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:32.400
documentary was even an added feature on&nbsp;
a Criterion laserdisc of Boogie Nights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:37.200
with Anderson pointing out in the commentary&nbsp;
how the film was a, quote, "major influence."

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:43.120
A final shocking fact to come out of Boogie&nbsp;
Nights is that its star, a man still known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:47.600
at the time as an underwear model and teen&nbsp;
idol called Marky Mark, has said that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:53.040
probably wouldn't do Boogie Nights again despite&nbsp;
having just a few acting credits at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:59.120
Yes, Mark Wahlberg said in a 2017 appearance that&nbsp;
he hopes God forgives him for his poor acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.680
choices, and that Boogie Nights is at the top&nbsp;
of the list. There was a lot of outcry about him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:08.640
negating the movie that gave him serious acting&nbsp;
cred. As Julianne Moore put it to Andy Cohen,

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:13.200
"Oh my God, I think he should thank God&nbsp;
for 'Boogie Nights.' It made his career!"

